WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Starch in bread and cellulose in wood are both pure glucose.

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.260
So why do you digest one, and not the other?

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.340
The answer is one bond, pointing one of two ways.

4
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.690
In water, a glucose chain does not stay straight.

5
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:23.430
The hydroxyl on its carbon five swings around and attacks carbon one.

6
00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.050
The ring closes on its own.

7
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:31.233
Carbon one becomes a new, special carbon: the anomeric carbon.

8
00:00:32.573 --> 00:00:35.893
Its new hydroxyl group can point to either face of the ring.

9
00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:41.513
β means that group sits on the same face as carbon six.

10
00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:43.383
α means the opposite face.

11
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:45.863
That is the snap to follow.

12
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:51.833
While that carbon is free, the ring keeps reopening, flipping between alpha and

13
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:55.713
beta. In water, about two thirds settle as beta.

14
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:00.133
Now bond that carbon to carbon four of the next glucose.

15
00:01:01.453 --> 00:01:03.433
Once used, the ring cannot open.

16
00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:05.953
The face is locked in.

17
00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:10.223
Starch and cellulose both use this 1,4 bond.

18
00:01:11.423 --> 00:01:13.303
Starch makes it from the α face.

19
00:01:14.703 --> 00:01:16.543
Cellulose makes it from the β face.

20
00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.947
Put simply, the real ring is puckered.

21
00:01:21.127 --> 00:01:25.107
In beta, the carbon one bond points straight across from carbon four.

22
00:01:26.407 --> 00:01:28.507
In alpha, it leaves at a sharp angle.

23
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.220
Now chain glucoses together each way.

24
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.420
Which chain runs straight?

25
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.320
Which one bends at every glucose?

26
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:39.840
Picture it first.

27
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:47.923
Beta runs straight, like a rod through beads.

28
00:01:49.223 --> 00:01:53.563
Each glucose sits flipped from its neighbors, so the chain lies flat, a ribbon.

29
00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:57.897
Alpha bends at every joint, always the same way.

30
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:04.397
So the chain winds into a helix, about six glucoses per turn in amylose, a starch.

31
00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.630
Flat beta ribbons lie side by side and hydrogen bond into rigid fibers.

32
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.330
That is cellulose, which holds plants up.

33
00:02:15.283 --> 00:02:18.603
Alpha helices cannot lie flat together that way.

34
00:02:19.743 --> 00:02:25.083
They pack into compact granules instead: starch in plants, glycogen in animals.

35
00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:27.583
Stored fuel.

36
00:02:28.183 --> 00:02:30.043
Shape is only part of the story.

37
00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:34.003
Digestion needs an enzyme that fits the bond.

38
00:02:35.323 --> 00:02:37.243
Your amylases fit α-1,4.

39
00:02:38.317 --> 00:02:42.037
You make no cellulase to cut the β bonds of cellulose.

40
00:02:43.237 --> 00:02:47.337
A cow lives on grass because its gut microbes make cellulase for it.

41
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.780
The brick never changes.

42
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.820
Only the face of one bond does.

43
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:59.580
For glucose joined 1 to 4, α coils into fuel and β runs straight into fiber.
